{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 399,
    "dDurationMs": 2490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to Brilliant for supporting PBS."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2889,
    "dDurationMs": 4451,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Paradoxically, the most promising prospects for moving matter around faster than light"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 7340,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "may be to put a metaphorical brick wall in its way."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 10700,
    "dDurationMs": 4950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "New efforts in quantum tunneling - both theory and experiment - show that superluminal motion"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 15650,
    "dDurationMs": 6289,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "may be possible, while still managing to avoid the paradox of superluminal signaling."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 26935,
    "dDurationMs": 4975,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Quantum tunneling is one of the weirder phenomena in the generally very weird world of quantum"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 31910,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mechanics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 32910,
    "dDurationMs": 4820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It describes how quantum particles are able\nto move across seemingly impenetrable barriers"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 37730,
    "dDurationMs": 2370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- for example, when atomic nuclei decay."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 40100,
    "dDurationMs": 5170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it’s not just the Houdini-like power\nthat makes the quantum world weird - it’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 45270,
    "dDurationMs": 5890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "also that the tunneling motion may move particles faster than they could travel if the barrier"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 51160,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wasn’t there - and even faster than light\ncould traverse the same distance."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 55920,
    "dDurationMs": 4870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now we covered quantum tunneling a long long time ago - in fact it was the first video"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 60790,
    "dDurationMs": 1880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we did on quantum mechanics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 62670,
    "dDurationMs": 3240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But things have actually evolved in the 5\nyears since."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 65910,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I mean, we’ve learned a lot on this show\nand so we can dig deeper into the FTL aspect"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 71390,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of quantum tunneling."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 72390,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there’s also new science, and so today\nwe’re going to look at a new theoretical"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 77670,
    "dDurationMs": 5790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "result and a new experiment that are bringing us closer to understanding the superluminal"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 83460,
    "dDurationMs": 2000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "prospects of quantum tunneling."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 85460,
    "dDurationMs": 2740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But first, a quick recap of quantum tunneling."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 88200,
    "dDurationMs": 4780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Imagine you’re driving a car towards a steep hill when the engine cuts out."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 92980,
    "dDurationMs": 3450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You can still make it over the hill if you\nhave enough speed - enough kinetic energy"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 96430,
    "dDurationMs": 1650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to see you to the top."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 98080,
    "dDurationMs": 4579,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But if the car isn’t moving fast enough\nthen it’ll inevitably slow down and roll"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 102659,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "back."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 103659,
    "dDurationMs": 3651,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s nothing in the laws of physics that\ncould allow you to reach the other side of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107310,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the hill."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 108310,
    "dDurationMs": 2970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, nothing in the laws of classical physics anyway."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 111280,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A similar thing happens in the world of quantum mechanics, where particles are pushed and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 115200,
    "dDurationMs": 5950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pulled by the fundamental forces, forming\nenergetic hills and valleys - a landscape"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 121150,
    "dDurationMs": 2190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of so-called potential barriers."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 123340,
    "dDurationMs": 5689,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, the protons and neutrons in an\natomic nucleus are held in the potential barrier"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 129029,
    "dDurationMs": 2550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the strong nuclear force."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 131579,
    "dDurationMs": 4630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If one of these particles had enough energy\nit could punch through that barrier."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 136209,
    "dDurationMs": 4911,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Fortunately for the stability of atoms, nucleons mostly remain trapped."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 141120,
    "dDurationMs": 1250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Mostly."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 142370,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In radioactive decay, particles that should never have enough energy to escape the nucleus"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 147450,
    "dDurationMs": 2579,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are found to leak out."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 150029,
    "dDurationMs": 1691,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is quantum tunneling."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 151720,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The key to the escape is quantum uncertainty."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 155480,
    "dDurationMs": 4780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Between observations, quantum particles don’t have well defined properties - and that includes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 160260,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their positions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 161260,
    "dDurationMs": 5039,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We represent the location of, say, a proton\nin a nucleus as a wavefunction."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 166299,
    "dDurationMs": 6291,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s an abstract wave that encodes the information of where the proton might be."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 172590,
    "dDurationMs": 4739,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Upon measurement, or upon interaction with another particle, the proton can end up anywhere"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 177329,
    "dDurationMs": 4490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "within that wavefunction, with some locations more likely than others."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 181819,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To understand what happens when a proton bounces around inside a nucleus, we need to see how "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 186319,
    "dDurationMs": 5711,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its wavefunction evolves according the the Schrodinger equation - which is just the equation"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 192030,
    "dDurationMs": 1810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of motion of wavefunctions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 193840,
    "dDurationMs": 6099,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This equation tells us that the wavefunction\nis mostly reflected or scattered back by the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 199939,
    "dDurationMs": 1060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wall of the nucleus."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 200999,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the Schrodinger equation is very clear\nthat this isn’t the only thing that can"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 205319,
    "dDurationMs": 1290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "happen."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 206609,
    "dDurationMs": 5590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Due to the blurred-out nature of the wavefunction, a small part of it leaks out through to the other"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 212199,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "side."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 213199,
    "dDurationMs": 4430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The proton ends up being simultaneously reflected back AND transmitted through the barrier."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 217629,
    "dDurationMs": 4511,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now the latter is very improbable - only as\nlikely as the tiny fraction of the wavefunction"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 222140,
    "dDurationMs": 2280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that peaks through that barrier."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 224420,
    "dDurationMs": 6149,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But improbable isn’t impossible, and so\nif you then observe the nucleus, it’ll “collapse”"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 230569,
    "dDurationMs": 4820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into one of those two states - either business as usual, or a nuclear decay."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 235389,
    "dDurationMs": 6770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We see tunneling everywhere, in radioactive decay of course, but this tunneling also drives"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 242159,
    "dDurationMs": 1750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "many other important processes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 243909,
    "dDurationMs": 6571,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s necessary for the nuclear fusion reactions that power the sun, in some biological processes,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 250480,
    "dDurationMs": 5129,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and it’s even a critical part of the working\nof transistors and other electronic components."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 255609,
    "dDurationMs": 4930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But even with the ubiquity of this phenomenon, we know very little about what happens during"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 260539,
    "dDurationMs": 2141,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the tunneling event itself."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 262680,
    "dDurationMs": 6479,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, is the transition of the particle from one side of the barrier to the other instantaneous,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 269159,
    "dDurationMs": 2121,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or does it take some time?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 271280,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It turns out it’s very hard to determine\nthe so-called tunneling time because, in the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 276440,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fuzzy world of quantum mechanics, it’s hard to even define what we mean by tunnelling"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 280440,
    "dDurationMs": 2970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "time or time for that matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 283410,
    "dDurationMs": 6050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One thing is clear at least - for a number\nof definitions of tunneling time, faster-than-light"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 289460,
    "dDurationMs": 2150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "movement, really does seem to be a thing."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 291610,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This was first shown by the physicist Thomas Hartman in 1962, who found that for one definition,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 297930,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the time taken to tunnel can become independent of the thickness of the barrier."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 302310,
    "dDurationMs": 4050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In other words, you can double the length\nof your barrier, and your particle will take"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 306360,
    "dDurationMs": 3370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the same amount of time to travel all the\nway through."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 309730,
    "dDurationMs": 5620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For a thick enough barrier, this ‘Hartman effect’ can effectively teleport real, physical"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 315350,
    "dDurationMs": 5219,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "matter between locations faster than it would take to travel that distance sans barrier"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 320569,
    "dDurationMs": 1781,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- even at the speed of light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 322350,
    "dDurationMs": 5499,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, our old pal Professor Einstein is not\na fan of faster-than-light motion."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 327849,
    "dDurationMs": 4940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As we’ve said before, his theory of special relativity explains that if you move faster"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 332789,
    "dDurationMs": 4881,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than light, you can send signals into the\npast, and create a whole bunch of paradoxes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 337670,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So in our previous tunneling episode, we offered an explanation for why this effect doesn’t"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 343970,
    "dDurationMs": 1580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "break relativity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 345550,
    "dDurationMs": 3350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It came down to the definition of tunneling\ntime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 348900,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If the position of the tunneling particle\nisn’t perfectly known, how do we know when"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 353260,
    "dDurationMs": 2790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to start and stop our tunneling stopwatch?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 356050,
    "dDurationMs": 4290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It seems natural to define those times as\nwhenever the center of the wavefunction passes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 360340,
    "dDurationMs": 1530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the start and end points."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 361870,
    "dDurationMs": 4150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But what if the wavefunction changes during the tunneling."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 366020,
    "dDurationMs": 6340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In a sense, the leading edge of the old wavefunction becomes the center of the new wavefunction."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 372360,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s like if you measured a train’s travel\ntime through a tunnel by clicking a stopwatch"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 378060,
    "dDurationMs": 4650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as the center of the train passed the entrance to the tunnel, and then again when the front"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 382710,
    "dDurationMs": 2190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the train reaches the exit."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 384900,
    "dDurationMs": 5380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You’ll get a shorter time than if you clicked\nfor the same point at both tunnel ends."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 390280,
    "dDurationMs": 5490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now if this was a quantum-tunneling train,\nthen only the front carriage would make it"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 395770,
    "dDurationMs": 3930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "through the tunnel, while the rest of the\ntrain would be reversed and travel back the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 399700,
    "dDurationMs": 1429,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "way it came."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 401129,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And then when you observe the train, all but\none of the carriages would vanish!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 405729,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s hard to measure the travel time of\na quantum train OR a quantum wavefunction"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 411849,
    "dDurationMs": 3241,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because it’s hard to define the start and\nend points."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 415090,
    "dDurationMs": 3039,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Certain definitions seem to imply faster than light motion."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 418129,
    "dDurationMs": 2901,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But that’s also true of motion without a\nbarrier."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 421030,
    "dDurationMs": 4030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Launch a particle through empty space with\na well defined starting position, and it’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 425060,
    "dDurationMs": 3810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "position wavefunction will spread out before the finish line."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 428870,
    "dDurationMs": 5210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The center of that wavefunction can’t travel\nfaster than the speed of light, but upon measurement,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 434080,
    "dDurationMs": 4930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the particle may appear to be at the leading\nedge of its wavefunction - potentially nudging"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 439010,
    "dDurationMs": 1770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it above light speed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 440780,
    "dDurationMs": 3539,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So you can see how the question of tunneling time is a bit messy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 444319,
    "dDurationMs": 4421,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If we want to answer this, we need to define\na better question."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 448740,
    "dDurationMs": 5649,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s instead ask the following: is it possible\nto send a message between two points that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 454389,
    "dDurationMs": 4421,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are separated by a barrier faster than you\ncan transmit the same message through empty"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 458810,
    "dDurationMs": 1160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "space?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 459970,
    "dDurationMs": 5909,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Fortunately a recent paper helps us answer\nexactly that from a theoretical standpoint."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 465879,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Most previous work on tunneling time relied\non the Schrodinger equation, which doesn’t"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 471319,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "incorporate Einsteins’s special theory of\nrelativity and so has no speed limit baked"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 475639,
    "dDurationMs": 1400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 477039,
    "dDurationMs": 5421,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These new guys use the Dirac equation, which properly incorporates special relativity and so we"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 482460,
    "dDurationMs": 4390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can take any emerging FTL motion more seriously."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 486850,
    "dDurationMs": 3140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The explanation boils down to a thought experiment."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 489990,
    "dDurationMs": 4859,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Imagine you try to send a message encoded in a collection of particles to a friend,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 494849,
    "dDurationMs": 2701,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and you want it to arrive as soon as possible."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 497550,
    "dDurationMs": 4730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Should you send the message through empty space, or through a barrier?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 502280,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Can quantum tunneling really speed up the\ntransmission of the information contained"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 506360,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in that message?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 507360,
    "dDurationMs": 4059,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well it turns out that the answer depends on what you mean by “receive the message”."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 511419,
    "dDurationMs": 4671,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you can count the message received at the instant the first particle arrives, then the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 516090,
    "dDurationMs": 4150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "new study finds that the tunneling message\nreally does arrive first."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 520240,
    "dDurationMs": 3580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the thicker the barrier, the bigger the\ndifference in arrival time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 523820,
    "dDurationMs": 6770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s just what Hartman calculated using\narguably the wrong equation back in 1962."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 530590,
    "dDurationMs": 3570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The study also finds that the tunneling wave\npacket isn’t necessarily “reshaped”"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 534160,
    "dDurationMs": 6290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all that much - it’s not clear that we can\nreally think of the new wavefunction as the cut-off front end"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 540450,
    "dDurationMs": 4170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the old wavefunction, or the first carriage of the quantum train."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 544620,
    "dDurationMs": 2860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So FTL verified, right?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 547480,
    "dDurationMs": 1920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Not quite."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 549400,
    "dDurationMs": 6350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The study finds that the average travel time\nfor tunneling particles is shorter than the average "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 555750,
    "dDurationMs": 1790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "time the free-flying particles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 557540,
    "dDurationMs": 4940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But that’s only for the tunneling particles\nthat make it through."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 562480,
    "dDurationMs": 5170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Most get reflected by the barrier, and as\nthe barrier gets thicker, exponentially more"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 567650,
    "dDurationMs": 4270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "get reflected until only a miniscule number\npass through."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 571920,
    "dDurationMs": 5620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you try to send your message over and over, your friend will most likely receive a free-flying"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 577540,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particle long before they receive a tunneling\nparticle - staggeringly more likely for any"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 583380,
    "dDurationMs": 4890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "meaningful distance, and that’s just because the former is much more likely to make it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 588270,
    "dDurationMs": 3230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Does this really save causality?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 591500,
    "dDurationMs": 5350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In order to violate causality, your friend\nwould need to send a return message that was"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 596850,
    "dDurationMs": 4870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "influenced by your message to them, which\ncould then cause a paradox loop."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 601720,
    "dDurationMs": 5300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The authors say that more work is needed to verify that this is ruled out, but in general"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 607020,
    "dDurationMs": 2150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it looks like a lifeline for causality."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 609170,
    "dDurationMs": 7140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "All this theoretical stuff is good and\nfun, but what does experiment have to say?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 616310,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Efforts from the early 80s and on seemed to agree that the Hartman effect is real, but"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 621670,
    "dDurationMs": 4340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "interpretation of the results suffer from\nsome of the same problems as the theoretical"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 626010,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "calculations - how do we define the tunneling time?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 629690,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And even trickier, how do we define a tunneling time that we can actually measure?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 635690,
    "dDurationMs": 4610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For a real, physical experiment, we need a\nclock that’s physically measurable."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 640300,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In 2020, a paper was published in the journal Nature that used the swiveling axis of a particle’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 646540,
    "dDurationMs": 2020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum spin as the clock hand."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 648560,
    "dDurationMs": 5470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The phenomenon is called Larmor precession, in which a particle’s dipole magnetic field,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 654030,
    "dDurationMs": 5380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which is defined by its spin axis, precesses\nlike a top in an external magnetic field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 659410,
    "dDurationMs": 3300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The rate of rotation can be used as an internal clock."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 662710,
    "dDurationMs": 6270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In this experiment, they fired ultracold\nrubidium atoms at a laser field that was spread"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 668980,
    "dDurationMs": 1820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out over a small area."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 670800,
    "dDurationMs": 5970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That field was strong enough to deflect the\natoms completely, and so provided an insurmountable"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 676770,
    "dDurationMs": 1180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "barrier."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 677950,
    "dDurationMs": 2730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Some particles, naturally, did manage to tunnel through anyway."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 680680,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For those ones, their spins were altered by\nthe magnetic field of the laser, and the longer"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 685520,
    "dDurationMs": 3080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they spent inside the barrier, the more their\nspins changed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 688600,
    "dDurationMs": 2380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, ok, what did they find?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 690980,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Did the particles travel faster than light?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 694100,
    "dDurationMs": 4180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well… no, but they weren’t trying to make\nthem do that."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 698280,
    "dDurationMs": 5540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They were just trying to verify whether using\nspins as an internal clock would work at all,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 703820,
    "dDurationMs": 1330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and they were successful."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 705150,
    "dDurationMs": 4330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The spins were altered by pretty much the\nsame amount that theory predicted they would"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 709480,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 710480,
    "dDurationMs": 2980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the results are still relevant for\nthe faster-than-light Hartman effect, because"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 713460,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the spin-based clocks that they were working with should still show the effect under FTL"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 719040,
    "dDurationMs": 3670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "circumstances, with faster particles and a\nthicker barrier."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 722710,
    "dDurationMs": 5770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The point is that the theory and the experimental tools are now converging on a way to answer"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 728480,
    "dDurationMs": 2650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our questions once and for all."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 731130,
    "dDurationMs": 3870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Is faster-than-light motion or influence possible?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 735000,
    "dDurationMs": 6860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Perhaps yes, but it seems only in cases where faster-than-light signaling is impossible."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 741860,
    "dDurationMs": 5270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Because as we’ve discussed many times before, when it comes to the speed of light, the house"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 747130,
    "dDurationMs": 1470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "always wins."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 748600,
    "dDurationMs": 4910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "All signals in our universe, whether via quantum tunneling or quantum entanglement, seem to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 753510,
    "dDurationMs": 3790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be bound by the same limits imposed by relativity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 757300,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The universe insists that we take the long\nway around, and as fast as we can find them"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 763420,
    "dDurationMs": 3890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it seals up any new shortcuts through spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 767310,
    "dDurationMs": 2560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to Brilliant for supporting PBS."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 769870,
    "dDurationMs": 4090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To understand astrophysics, Brilliant believes it helps to have a solid understanding of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 773960,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "relativity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 774960,
    "dDurationMs": 4770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Brilliant has a lesson on special relativity\nthat includes interactive challenges and problems"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 779730,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to solve."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 780730,
    "dDurationMs": 4230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A hands-on approach can guide you through thinking strategies for challenging subjects"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 784960,
    "dDurationMs": 1870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like relativity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 786830,
    "dDurationMs": 4140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It starts out explaining relativity in terms\nof boats passing by each other on a river,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 790970,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then goes into some puzzles that require challenging perspective (like the ball stack bounce),"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 796530,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and then goes into the speed of light and\neven time travel!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 800850,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To learn more about Brilliant, go to brilliant.org/Spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 805570,
    "dDurationMs": 4030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As always, if you've joined us on Patreon,\nI can't emphasize enough how helpful your"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 809600,
    "dDurationMs": 2680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "support is in keeping this show going."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 812280,
    "dDurationMs": 6070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But today I want to give a huge shoutout to\nour Big Bang supporter Henry Van Styn."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 818350,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Henry, as a scientist I may not know everything\nabout the depths of quantum tunneling, but"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 824030,
    "dDurationMs": 6930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as a New Yorker I know that every tunnel needs a name so I’m proud to announce the Henry"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 830960,
    "dDurationMs": 2340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Van Styn Quantum Tunnel."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 833300,
    "dDurationMs": 5370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It connects downtown Manhattan to … well,\nwherever you live right now so we can hang"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 838670,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 839670,
    "dDurationMs": 3540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It goes through solid bedrock so there’s a low probability that your"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 843210,
    "dDurationMs": 5050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum wavefunction will make it through\n- but if you do then the trip is instantaneous,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 848260,
    "dDurationMs": 2340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and so you get to avoid the downtown traffic."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 850600,
    "dDurationMs": 3390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Henry, you have our thanks and now you have our tunnel."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 853990,
    "dDurationMs": 1460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Please visit soon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 855450,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the last episode we talked about why physicists believe magnetic monopoles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 859950,
    "dDurationMs": 6220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You guys had your own strong opinions of questions and lots of questions. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 866170,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "MrRolnichek asks about the hedgehogs configurations - the radiating knots in the Higgs field that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 871370,
    "dDurationMs": 2190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "might lead to magnetic monopoles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 873560,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They ask — if these can’t be removed by\nsmooth variation in the vectors then how come"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 880360,
    "dDurationMs": 2930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they're created in high energy events?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 883290,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The answer is that at very high energies,\nthe direction of the Higgs field becomes free"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 887770,
    "dDurationMs": 5260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to vary wildly and adjacent points in the\nfield are less coupled to each other."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 893030,
    "dDurationMs": 4350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Only when the field cools down to adjacent\npoints become more tied to each other."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 897380,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Any discontinuities that formed at high temperature can then be frozen into the field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 903300,
    "dDurationMs": 6670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And Nate Underwood asks if the Higgs field\ncan form so that there are no monopoles - no"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 909970,
    "dDurationMs": 1260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "discontinuities."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 911230,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The answer is yes, but it’s extremely unlikely-\n- for the same reason that it’s increasingly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 915850,
    "dDurationMs": 4190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "unlikely for a crystal to have no flaws the\nlarger it is."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 920040,
    "dDurationMs": 3050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Try to make a flawless crystal the size of\nthe universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 923090,
    "dDurationMs": 4910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Erik says that if magnetic monopoles are massive enough to collapse the early univeres, wouldn’t"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 928000,
    "dDurationMs": 2300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we only find them inside black holes?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 930300,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well it depends on their density and whether inflation threw them apart faster than they"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 935640,
    "dDurationMs": 1130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "could find each other."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 936770,
    "dDurationMs": 5500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s certainly possible that magnetic monopoles to end up inside black holes, in fact some recent"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 942270,
    "dDurationMs": 3950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "work suggests that an individual magnetic\nmonopole would manifest as a black hole - in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 946220,
    "dDurationMs": 2050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that it would have an event horizon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 948270,
    "dDurationMs": 3900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However a monopole managed to get into a black hole, one thing is clear - it would still"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 952170,
    "dDurationMs": 5060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "behave like a monopole in that the black hole would radiate magnetic field lines."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 957230,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Same as if the black hole held electric charge; it would produce an electric field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 961790,
    "dDurationMs": 4580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And presumably if black holes had enormousmagnetic charges we’d see that in the way"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 966370,
    "dDurationMs": 1850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they interact with matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 968220,
    "dDurationMs": 4940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As it is, the magnetic fields we observe around black holes seem more consistent with regular"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 973160,
    "dDurationMs": 2370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dipole fields, with both north and south poles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 975530,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example in the way they channel jets of\nmaterial in opposite directions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 980510,
    "dDurationMs": 2800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Wasko92 asks the real question."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 983310,
    "dDurationMs": 5030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Magnetic monopoles aside, why did Moody arrange for the whole Goblet of Fire thing when he could"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 988340,
    "dDurationMs": 4530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have easily taken some of Harry’s blood on one of the numerous occasions that they met."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 992870,
    "dDurationMs": 5030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK wasko92, I know this was supposed to be some hilarious non-sequitur with no relation"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 997900,
    "dDurationMs": 5818,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the episode, but the jokes is on you my friend, because it's relevant to this episode"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1003718,
    "dDurationMs": 5402,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As everyone knows, Moody tricked Harry into touching the tri-wizard cup which was actually"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1009120,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "enchanted into a port key that transported\nhim and poor Cedric off Hogwart's campus."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1015060,
    "dDurationMs": 7220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It's hypothesized that portkeys are based on wormhole technology, but actually they may well use"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1022280,
    "dDurationMs": 1720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum tunneling."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1024000,
    "dDurationMs": 5900,
    "segs": [ {
